# Sign-in by password, held by Caddy. THE DEFAULT, and the one that works on a
# server with nothing else on it.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The other snippet hands identity off to oauth2-proxy and an
# identity provider, which is right for an install that already has one and is
# an absurd prerequisite for somebody who wants to run this for their own pool.
# Nothing here needs a second container, a tenant, or a registered application.
#
# HOW IDENTITY REACHES THE APPLICATION. poolhound learns who you are from
# X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL-NAME and decides what you may do in access.py. That
# header is a seam, not an Entra detail: anything that can prove who somebody
# is and set it works, and `{http.auth.user.id}` is the username basic_auth
# just verified. So access.py's roles are keyed on the usernames below with no
# change to a line of Python.
#
# THE HEADER IS SET AFTER THE CHECK, NEVER BEFORE. The vhost deletes every
# identity header from the incoming request first, before anything has had a
# chance to authenticate; this sets it only on the far side of basic_auth. A
# `route` block preserves written order, which is what makes that sequence
# guaranteed rather than hopeful.
#
# ADDING A USER: generate a hash and write one `username hash` line per person
# into deploy/caddy/users.caddy, which is gitignored.
#
#     docker run --rm caddy:2 caddy hash-password --plaintext 'your password' \
#       | sed "s|^|owner |" >> deploy/caddy/users.caddy
#
# A FILE, NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, and that is not a style preference. A
# bcrypt hash is `$2a$14$...` — three dollar signs — and docker compose
# interpolates `$VAR` in an env file, so passing the hash that way silently
# truncates it to `owner a4` and every correct password is rejected with a 401
# that looks exactly like a wrong one. MEASURED: the first version of this file
# did it that way and the end-to-end test refused the right password.
#
# Missing or empty, NOBODY can sign in — which is the right failure for a
# forgotten setting, and leaves the public half of the site working.
#
# The username is what access.py matches, so it is the name to put in
# config.toml's [access] section:
#
#     [access]
#     admins = ["you"]
(poolhound_auth) {
	basic_auth {
		# One `username hash` line per person, spliced in verbatim. The file
		# is gitignored; compose mounts it read-only.
		import /etc/caddy/users.caddy
	}
	# The name basic_auth just verified, handed to the application as identity.
	request_header X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL-NAME {http.auth.user.id}
}

# IDENTITY WITHOUT REQUIRING IT — see the note in auth-forward.caddy.
#
# Here credentials ARE the Authorization header, so its presence is the
# question: basic_auth challenges a caller who sent nothing, and the PUBLIC
# page calls /api/health before anybody has signed in, which would put a
# browser password box in front of the volume calculator. Absent means
# anonymous; present means checked. A WRONG password is still refused — only
# the absence of one is waved past, and poolhound issues an anonymous caller
# no write token.
(poolhound_identity) {
	@offered header Authorization *
	handle @offered {
		import poolhound_auth
		reverse_proxy poolhound:8787
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy poolhound:8787
	}
}
